WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.199 --> 00:00:08.199
The next material optimization I'd like to talk about are Material Quality Expression Nodes.

2
00:00:08.199 --> 00:00:15.199
Material Quality Expression Nodes allow you to define how your material is going to react or look

3
00:00:15.199 --> 00:00:23.199
based on the shading path, feature level, or quality setting that is selected inside of your project.

4
00:00:23.199 --> 00:00:34.899
The shading path switch allows you to define how your material will react when used on a deferred, forward, or mobile renderer.

5
00:00:34.899 --> 00:00:42.899
This allows you to anticipate things not necessarily being supported instead of the end user receiving an error.

6
00:00:42.899 --> 00:00:53.409
They get a different version of the material that works with the hardware that is available on that platform.

7
00:00:53.409 --> 00:01:06.439
Feature level does something similar, but it allows us to define by feature level, such as Embedded System 2, Embedded System 3, Shader Model 5, or Shader Model 6.

8
00:01:06.439 --> 00:01:18.439
The quality feature switch allows us to define how this material will look when different quality levels are selected inside of the project settings.

9
00:01:18.439 --> 00:01:25.439
With this, we can have a default and then define various materials for low, high, medium, and epic settings.

10
00:01:25.439 --> 00:01:35.439
Depending on your target platform or target platforms, you may or may not need to utilize the shading path, feature level, or quality switch nodes.

11
00:01:35.439 --> 00:01:46.439
However, this is something that you should research for your project, as quality switches enable you to ensure your project will look how you want,

12
00:01:46.439 --> 00:01:55.439
even when run on a platform that might not necessarily have the hardware needed to display your materials in the manner you wish.
